---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import { url } from "../lib/site";
---

<Layout
	title="Privacy Policy - NoteGuard"
	description="How NoteGuard handles your data: nothing is collected, nothing is uploaded, every AI model runs on your device, and only links you paste are fetched."
	breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Privacy Policy", path: "/privacy-policy/" }]}
>
	<main>
		<PageHeader
			eyebrow="Legal"
			title="Privacy Policy"
			lede="NoteGuard is built so that there is nothing to collect. This page explains what the app does with your data, which is very close to nothing."
			meta="Last updated: November 10, 2026."
		/>

		<div class="container mx-auto px-6 max-w-3xl pb-24">
			<div class="legal">
				<section class="mb-10">
					<h2>Introduction</h2>
					<p>
						NoteGuard ("we", "our" or "us") is a note-taking application
						for Android. This Privacy Policy explains how information is
						handled when you use the NoteGuard app and this website.
					</p>
					<p>
						<strong>Our core position is simple: your data belongs to
						you.</strong> NoteGuard is a local-first application. There
						is no account to create, no server that receives your
						notes, and no analytics service watching how you use the
						app.
					</p>
				</section>

				<section class="mb-10">
					<h2>What we collect</h2>
					<p>
						<strong>Nothing.</strong> We do not collect personal
						information, usage data, analytics, crash reports,
						advertising identifiers or device identifiers. There are
						no third-party tracking or analytics SDKs in the app.
					</p>
					<p>
						Your notes, attachments, checklists and settings are
						written to storage on your own device. They are not
						uploaded, and they are not transmitted to us, because
						there is no account and no backend to transmit them to.
					</p>
				</section>

				<section class="mb-10">
					<h2>Where your notes are stored</h2>
					<p>
						NoteGuard stores each note as a plain Markdown file, with
						metadata in frontmatter, inside a vault folder that you
						choose on your device. Notes that are not locked are
						ordinary files and can be read by any app or tool with
						access to that folder.
					</p>
					<p>
						Android's automatic app-data backup is disabled for
						NoteGuard, so your vault is not shipped to Google Drive
						by the operating system. Backups are made by you, by
						copying the vault folder or by exporting a file.
					</p>
				</section>

				<section class="mb-10">
					<h2>Encryption and your PIN</h2>
					<p>
						When you lock a note, its text, checklist items and
						attachments are encrypted before they reach the disk:
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>
							The key is derived from the PIN you type using
							<strong>PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256</strong> with 210,000
							rounds, one derivation per note.
						</li>
						<li>
							Note text is encrypted with <strong>AES-256-CBC</strong>
							and authenticated with an <strong>HMAC-SHA256</strong>
							tag that is verified before anything is decrypted.
						</li>
						<li>
							Photos, voice memos and drawings are sealed as
							<strong>AES-256-GCM</strong> frames, so a note is
							never only partly protected.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong>Your PIN is not stored anywhere.</strong> No
							copy of it exists to compare against, so there is no
							reset, no recovery code and no way for us — or for
							anyone else — to recover a locked note without it.
						</li>
						<li>
							Biometrics are a shortcut rather than the key. When
							biometric unlock is enabled, a copy of the PIN is
							held in the device's secure keystore so a
							fingerprint or face can enter it for you. Turning
							biometrics off deletes every such copy.
						</li>
						<li>
							Nothing locked is shown anywhere — not in lists,
							search, the home-screen widget, notifications or the
							on-device AI — and a locked note's text is never sent
							to a model, even one running on the same device.
						</li>
					</ul>
					<p>
						The app lock is separate from a note lock. Its PIN is
						stored only as a PBKDF2 hash, verified in constant time,
						behind a brute-force throttle that survives restarts.
					</p>
				</section>

				<section class="mb-10">
					<h2>On-device AI and the one network request</h2>
					<p>
						Every AI feature — chat, summarisation, title generation,
						writing, transcription, semantic search, image search
						and image generation — runs on your device using models
						you download from inside the app. Model files are
						fetched from the model publisher's distribution hosting;
						prompts, notes and images are never uploaded.
					</p>
					<p>
						<strong
							>The one exception: if you paste a web link and ask
							the app to summarise it, that single page is
							fetched.</strong
						>
						Nothing else is ever requested, and web search is
						deliberately not a feature of the app.
					</p>
				</section>

				<section class="mb-10">
					<h2>Permissions</h2>
					<p>
						NoteGuard asks for permissions only when you use the
						feature that needs them:
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>
							<strong>Microphone</strong> — to record voice memos and
							to dictate. Recordings are processed on the device
							and are never uploaded.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong>Camera and photos</strong> — to attach or take
							photos and videos, and to scan a document.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong>Files and storage</strong> — to read and write
							your vault folder, to import Markdown files and to
							write exports.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong>Biometrics</strong> — only if you turn on
							biometric unlock. Fingerprint and face data stay
							with the device's secure hardware and are never
							accessible to the app.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong>Notifications</strong> — for task reminders and
							for a note you have chosen to stick to the
							notification shade.
						</li>
					</ul>
				</section>

				<section class="mb-10">
					<h2>Exports, backups and deletion</h2>
					<p>
						Exports and backups are created on your device and then
						handed to you — a file you save, share or upload
						wherever you like. That is where responsibility for the
						copy sits: once a file leaves the app, it is governed by
						whatever you shared it with.
					</p>
					<p>
						Deleting a note moves it to the recycle bin, where it is
						kept for 30 days before being deleted permanently.
						Restoring it returns it to where it was.
					</p>
				</section>

				<section class="mb-10">
					<h2>Children</h2>
					<p>
						NoteGuard is a general-purpose productivity tool and is
						not directed at children. Because the app collects no
						personal information at all, it does not knowingly
						collect information from anyone, including children
						under 13.
					</p>
				</section>

				<section class="mb-10">
					<h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
					<p>
						This policy may be updated as the app changes. The
						current version is always published on this page, with
						the date it was last updated. Material changes will be
						mentioned in the app's release notes.
					</p>
				</section>

				<section class="mb-10">
					<h2>Contact</h2>
					<p>
						Questions about this policy, or a privacy problem you
						want to report, can be raised as an issue on the
						project's GitHub page at
						<a href="https://github.com/heymeowcat" rel="noopener"
							>github.com/heymeowcat</a
						>.
					</p>
				</section>
			</div>

			<a
				href={url("/")}
				class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-purple-300 hover:text-purple-200 transition-colors"
			>
				← Back to NoteGuard
			</a>
		</div>
	</main>
	<Footer />
</Layout>
